+# Task: secrets repository (P02-12) · Phase: 02 · Owner: builder (wt-p02s5)
+
+<!-- Save as platewise/docs/notes/P<nn>/<slug>.md. Write everything above "Compare checklist"
+BEFORE writing code, in your own words. Never paste or paraphrase the reference line by line. -->
+
+Read (reference @ `10e14b67`): `A/data/KeyStore.kt` (all), `I/Services/KeychainHelper.swift` (all),
+the key mask helper at the end of `A/ui/settings/SettingsViewModel.kt` (to see what a saved key
+shows); no reference tests cover either store. Research spec `03-ai-providers.md` §3.12 and §6
+R03.20, R03.27; `06-settings-data.md` §3.18 (secret store corruption), §3.19 and §5 (secret
+storage on the web); decisions D-16.7, D-16.51 (`16-what-we-change.md` row D-16.49 … D-16.66 and
+`18-phase-wise-build-plan.md` "Proposed decisions"); naming `17-naming-and-glossary.md` (table
+`secrets`, slots `<providerId>` and `fdc`). Acceptance ACC-E14-1. No Evidence ids.
+
+What it does (own words): the person's API keys (one per AI provider, plus their own FoodData
+Central key) live in the `secrets` table, never in settings, exports or backups. Each value is
+encrypted with AES-GCM under one device key: a 256-bit key made by Web Crypto as non-extractable,
+created the first time a key is saved and kept in `meta` as a stored CryptoKey (IndexedDB keeps it
+by structured clone; script can use it but never read its bytes). This only protects copies of the
+raw storage files; code running on the site can still use the key, which Settings says (D-16.7).
+Saving trims the value (a pasted newline breaks auth, R03.20); a blank value deletes the slot. The
+store must never stop the app from starting (R03.27): when the device key is missing or unusable,
+or a stored value cannot be decrypted, every slot reads as unset and the table is emptied so the
+person enters their keys again. Settings shows a saved key masked: its first four characters, an
+ellipsis and its last four (ACC-E14-1); short values show dots only.
+
+Inputs → outputs:
+- `src/data/crypto.ts`: `createDeviceKey(subtle)` → a new non-extractable AES-GCM 256 key;
+  `isDeviceKey(value)` → whether a stored value is a usable key (cross-realm safe);
+  `seal(subtle, key, text)` → `{ iv, data }` (fresh 12-byte IV each time); `unseal(subtle, key,
+  sealed)` → the text, or null when it cannot be decrypted; `readSealed(raw)` → the stored
+  `{ iv, data }` or null; `loadDeviceKey(db)` → the stored key or null; `ensureDeviceKey(db,
+  subtle)` → the stored key, creating it once (atomic, also across tabs); `webCrypto()` → the
+  browser's `SubtleCrypto` or null (insecure page).
+- `src/data/repos/secretsRepo.ts`: `createSecretsRepo(db, options?)` → `{ set(slot, value),
+  get(slot), slots() }`; `mask(value)`; `SECRET_SLOTS`, `isSecretSlot`, `SecretSlot`.
+
+Files: created `src/data/crypto.ts`, `src/data/crypto.test.ts`, `src/data/repos/secretsRepo.ts`,
+`src/data/repos/secretsRepo.test.ts`, this note.
+
+Edge cases / failure modes:
+- No device key yet and nothing stored → `get` null, `slots` [], nothing written.
+- Device key deleted (or replaced by something that is not a usable key) while values are stored →
+  `get` null for every slot, `slots` [], the table is emptied (and an unusable key value removed);
+  no throw. The next `set` creates a new key.
+- A stored value that fails to decrypt (damaged bytes, wrong key, bad shape) → null for every slot
+  and the table is emptied; the clearing transaction first checks the failing row is still the one
+  that failed, so a value another tab just saved over it is not wiped.
+- Two saves at once before any key exists → both use the one key that was stored first (the key
+  is generated outside the transaction, and the transaction keeps an existing usable key instead
+  of the new one). Creating a key also empties the table: values sealed by a lost key can never be
+  read again.
+- No Web Crypto (page not served over https) → `get` null and `slots` [] without clearing anything
+  (the values are fine, this page just cannot open them); `set` of a non-blank value rejects with
+  `SecretsUnavailableError`. Blank `set` still deletes.
+- `set` with a slot that is not a provider id or `fdc` → RangeError, nothing written.
+- " sk-abc \n" is stored as "sk-abc"; "   " deletes.
+- `mask`: 40 characters → 4 + "…" + 4; 8 characters or fewer → a fixed row of 8 dots (the length
+  is not shown); empty → empty.
+
+Our design differences / decisions:
+- Web Crypto AES-GCM under a non-extractable device key (D-16.51) instead of an OS keystore; the
+  stored row is `{ id: slot, iv, data }` (both `Uint8Array`), the key is the `meta` row
+  `deviceKey`.
+- Slots are our provider ids plus `fdc` (17-naming); the reference's speech keys, challenge token
+  and Drive token have no v1 slot.
+- Every value is trimmed on save for every slot (the reference trims only the food database key
+  on Android, R03.20).
+- Recovery is per read, not at app start: the first `get`/`slots` that meets a missing key or an
+  undecryptable value empties the table. The reference wipes and rebuilds its whole key file when
+  it fails to open.
+- The secrets table does not go through the record guard's quarantine. R03.27 and D-16.51 give it
+  its own recovery (empty and re-enter), and copying secret material into `quarantine` would put it
+  in a second table that export, repair or backup code may read; the "unreadable data" banner is
+  not raised for it. The device key in `meta` is handled the same way (an unusable value is
+  replaced, not kept aside). Writes still go only through `transact()`.
+- No live feed: the settings screens read `slots()`/`get()` when they open and after a save
+  (recovery writes, and a live query may not write).
+- `mask` is ours: the reference shows short keys in full; we show dots only.
+
+Tests we will write:
+- crypto: the key is AES-GCM 256, non-extractable (export refused), encrypt and decrypt only; seal
+  → unseal round trip; two seals of one text use different IVs and data; a damaged byte or another
+  key → null; the key survives storage in `meta` and still decrypts after a reopen;
+  `ensureDeviceKey` creates once (two parallel calls end on one stored key) and empties the table
+  when it creates; `readSealed` refuses bad shapes.
+- repository: round trip; " sk-abc \n" stored as "sk-abc"; blank deletes; stored bytes do not hold
+  the plain key; deleting the device key → `get` null for all, table empty, no throw (R03.27); an
+  undecryptable value → every slot unset and the table empty; a value saved over a failing one in
+  between is not wiped; `slots` lists slot names only, in a fixed order; unknown slot refused; no
+  Web Crypto → reads unset and nothing cleared, `set` rejects; two parallel first saves both read
+  back.
+- `mask` of a 40-character key shows 4 + … + 4 (ACC-E14-1); short and empty values.
+
+Compare checklist (filled after the rewrite; reopened `A/data/KeyStore.kt`,
+`I/Services/KeychainHelper.swift`, `A/ui/settings/SettingsViewModel.kt` mask helper):
+- [ ] Saving blank deletes the slot (tested in `<file>`)
+- [ ] Keys trimmed on save (tested in `<file>`)
+- [ ] A store that cannot be opened starts empty instead of crashing (tested in `<file>`)
+- [ ] Masked display: first 4, ellipsis, last 4 (tested in `<file>`)
+- Deliberate differences: <…>
+- Not taken: <…>
